@*
    The two productivity scores, and — the part that earns them — what each is
    made of.

    A score is a claim, not a measurement. Nobody can check "68" against anything,
    so a bare score is either taken on faith or ignored, and it is usually ignored.
    Handing the component the inputs rather than the number turns it into an
    argument somebody can join: a reader who thinks throughput is over-weighted can
    see it is worth three of the five and say so.

    Two cards rather than one, because one was unreadable. The old figure folded
    counts read against a moving bar together with proportions read against a fixed
    one, so it could fall because less shipped or because what shipped came back
    from review, and nothing on the card said which. Volume is the counts, against
    your own record; Quality is the proportions, against the window itself. They
    answer different questions and are drawn apart so they can be read apart.

    The same reasoning is why the note names the target. Showing "441 of 6" was the
    defect this part was rebuilt for, and the fix is not simply a bigger denominator
    — it is a denominator the reader can trace. Full marks is a quarter above their
    own busiest four weeks, so the note says which four weeks those were and what
    was merged in them. A target nobody can see is exactly as unarguable as the
    constant it replaced.

    The note also says what is NOT being scored, in the two ways that can happen:
    when there is no history to set a volume target from, and when the window itself
    came back truncated. Assistant sessions are scored in neither card — they count
    effort rather than output — and the note says that too, because a reader of the
    previous version will look for the row.

    Value is deliberately not passed. MetricScore works the figure out from the
    inputs it is given, so the number on screen cannot drift from the composition
    printed underneath it — which is a property worth more than saving the
    component the arithmetic. The module computes its own score for the trend
    series, and a unit test holds the two formulas to the same answer.
*@
@inherits DashboardPartBase<ProductivityScoreInsight>
@inject IProductivityInsights Insights

<PartFrame Title="Score"
           Note="@Note"
           Busy="Status == MetricStatusKind.Loading"
           OnRefresh="RefreshAsync"
           TestId="dashboard-score">
    <div class="dashboard-part__stack">
        @* The volume card carries the part's status, so a part that is loading or
           refused shows one status rather than the same sentence twice. The quality
           card is drawn only once there is something to draw: an empty composition
           under a status message would be a second empty state under the first.

           Once the part is ready, each card gets its own status, because either
           composition can be empty on its own — volume with no history to set a bar
           from, quality with nothing reviewed or sized. Left on Ready, the component
           would say the number was "scored from inputs this view does not show",
           which is the opposite of what happened: nothing was scored at all. *@
        <MetricScore Label="@VolumeLabel"
                     Components="@VolumeInputs"
                     Bands="Bands"
                     Status="@CardStatus(VolumeInputs)"
                     StatusMessage="@(IsReady ? VolumeEmpty : StatusMessage)"
                     Footnote="@VolumeFootnote"
                     TestId="dashboard-score-volume" />
        @if (IsReady)
        {
            <MetricScore Label="Quality"
                         Components="@QualityInputs"
                         Bands="Bands"
                         Status="@CardStatus(QualityInputs)"
                         StatusMessage="@QualityEmpty"
                         Footnote="@QualityFootnote"
                         TestId="dashboard-score-quality" />
        }
    </div>
</PartFrame>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The bands the scores are read against — floors only, so there are no ceilings
    /// to keep in step with the next band up.
    /// <para>
    /// Product copy rather than domain, which is why they live in the part and not
    /// in the module: what counts as "strong" is a judgement about how to talk to
    /// the reader, and moving it behind the port would make it look like a
    /// derivation.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly MetricBand[] Bands =
    [
        new("Quiet", 0m),
        new("Steady", 40m),
        new("Strong", 65m),
        new("Exceptional", 85m)
    ];

    private string VolumeLabel => Scope.IsAllRepositories
        ? "Volume — every repository"
        : $"Volume — {Scope.RepositoryAlias}";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the figures cover, where full marks came from, and every way in which
    /// the answer is less than the whole truth. Assembled clause by clause rather
    /// than written as one sentence, because most of the clauses are conditional and
    /// a note that claimed a floor was a total is the failure worth avoiding.
    /// </summary>
    private string Note
    {
        get
        {
            var note = $"Over {Scope.Weeks} weeks. Two scores, each weighted and normalised to 100:"
                + " volume is what you shipped against your own record, quality is how much of it"
                + " was reviewed promptly, merged clean, synced without a conflict and kept small.";

            if (IsReady)
            {
                note += " " + TargetSentence;

                if (!Value!.Complete)
                {
                    note += " Some of this window could not be read, so every count here is a floor"
                        + " and both scores are read from floors.";
                }
            }

            // Last, and present whether or not there are figures. It is the one
            // caveat here that is a fact about the composition rather than about
            // this reading.
            return note + " " + SessionsRefusal;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Where full marks came from, named with the block that set it — or, when
    /// nothing set it, the admission that the volume card is not being scored at
    /// all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The absence has to be said out loud rather than left as an empty card. A
    /// reader who cannot see that volume dropped out reads the quality score as the
    /// score of everything, which is a quieter version of the same dishonesty the
    /// fixed target was.
    /// </remarks>
    private string TargetSentence
    {
        get
        {
            if (Value!.Target is not { } target)
            {
                return "Volume is not being scored:"
                    + " your own history could not be read, or there is not enough of it yet,"
                    + " and there is no figure worth inventing in its place.";
            }

            var sentence = $"Full marks for volume is a quarter above your own best four weeks —"
                + $" {DashboardFormat.Whole(target.FullMarks)} merged pull requests over this window,"
                + $" from the {target.MergedPullRequests} you merged between"
                + $" {DashboardFormat.Day(target.From)} and {DashboardFormat.Day(target.To)}.";

            return Value!.TargetComplete
                ? sentence
                : sentence + " Part of that history could not be read, so the record — and the target"
                    + " built on it — is a floor.";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Why there is no sessions row. Permanent rather than conditional, because a
    /// reader of the previous version will look for it, and a missing row is
    /// indistinguishable from a row that dropped out for lack of data.
    /// </summary>
    private const string SessionsRefusal =
        "Assistant sessions are scored in neither: they count effort rather than output,"
        + " and are read in their own section below.";

    /// <summary>
    /// The footnote covers the targets as well as the weights. It was true of the
    /// weights alone only while the targets were constants nobody could see; a
    /// target derived from the reader's own record is exactly the kind of judgement
    /// this sentence exists to invite an argument about.
    /// </summary>
    private const string VolumeFootnote =
        "Weights and targets are a starting position. They are on screen because they are arguable.";

    private const string QualityFootnote =
        "Each input is a share of the pull requests that could have counted. Weights are arguable.";

    /// <summary>The part's own status until it is ready; then the card's, which is
    /// empty exactly when its composition is.</summary>
    private MetricStatusKind CardStatus(IReadOnlyList<MetricScoreComponent> inputs) =>
        IsReady && inputs.Count == 0 ? MetricStatusKind.Empty : Status;

    private const string VolumeEmpty =
        "Not scored: there is no history to set a target from.";

    private const string QualityEmpty =
        "Not scored: nothing in this window was reviewed or had its size read.";

    private IReadOnlyList<MetricScoreComponent> VolumeInputs =>
        IsReady ? Components(Value!.Volume) : [];

    private IReadOnlyList<MetricScoreComponent> QualityInputs =>
        IsReady ? Components(Value!.Quality) : [];

    private static IReadOnlyList<MetricScoreComponent> Components(ProductivityScore score) =>
        [.. score.Inputs.Select(input =>
            new MetricScoreComponent(input.Label, input.Value, input.Max, input.Weight))];

    protected override Task<InsightResult<ProductivityScoreInsight>> FetchAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
        Insights.GetScoreAsync(Scope, cancellationToken);

    /// <summary>
    /// Empty when there were no inputs at all — a window in which nothing happened
    /// that any of the measures could read. A score of zero over real inputs is a
    /// score, not an absence, and it renders as one.
    /// </summary>
    protected override bool IsEmpty(ProductivityScoreInsight value) => !value.HasInputs;

    protected override string EmptyMessage =>
        "Nothing happened in this window that any of the seven inputs could read.";

    protected override void InvalidateSource() => Insights.Invalidate(Scope);
}
